The epidemiology of happiness shows direct health effects: happy people live 7–10 years longer, have lower cardiovascular disease risk, and recover faster from surgery — happiness is literally life-extending.
Social capital — trust, reciprocity, community engagement — is both a component and a cause of happiness; high-happiness societies (Scandinavia) also have the most effective public institutions.
The World Health Organization now includes wellbeing (positive mental health) alongside the absence of disease in its definition of health — reflecting scientific consensus that happiness is a legitimate health outcome.
